{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794136528,"nonce":"BNIeqVtb1hxJXcEsc1R+eUqHzyQ=","title":"Dependency diet: make the default blinc build lighter to download and faster to compile","message":"Measured on the facade crate with default features (windowed), resolved from a clean consumer crate against the workspace lockfile; sizes are crates.io download and unpacked source.\n\nTotals: macOS 209 registry crates, 22.7 MB download, 123 MB unpacked. Linux 223 crates, 25.3 MB, 148 MB. Windows 203 crates, 37.0 MB, 299 MB (the windows 0.58 crate alone is 9.7 MB download and 123 MB unpacked, needed by wgpu-hal and gpu-allocator for DX12). Blinc's own 27 published crates are 22 MB uncompressed; the largest are blinc_tabler_icons 5.4 MB (optional), blinc_layout 4.4 MB, blinc_gpu 3.5 MB.\n\nClean debug build of the facade, 4 jobs, Apple silicon: 61 s wall, 209 s CPU over 273 units. Blinc's own crates are 33 s of the CPU time; the rest is dependencies. Slowest: naga 12.5 s, objc2-foundation 12.3 s (plus a second version 2.8 s), blinc_layout 10.3, wgpu-core 8.8, ash 8.2, read-fonts 6.0, moxcms 4.5, wgpu-hal 4.1, image 3.6, zerocopy 3.5, serde_core 3.4, metal 3.3, syn 3.2 and 2.2 (two versions), skrifa 2.9, swash 2.8, nom 2.6, regex-automata 2.3, toml_edit 2.2, rustybuzz 2.1 twice, usvg 2.1. Duplicate versions in the macOS tree: 19 (bitflags, block2, core-foundation, hashbrown, miniz_oxide, objc2 and its app-kit/foundation, png, rustc-hash, rustybuzz, syn, toml_datetime, toml_edit, ttf-parser, unicode-bidi-mirroring, unicode-ccc, winnow).\n\nVerified: the unused objc2 and objc2-foundation declarations in blinc_platform_desktop enabled every Foundation class binding; removing them takes objc2-foundation 0.3.2 from 12.3 s to 1.7 s on macOS.\n\nRanked actions, cheapest first:\n1. Remove declared-but-unreferenced deps (source grep, 45 flagged repo-wide; in the facade path): blinc_theme toml and serde (drops toml, toml_edit, toml_datetime, serde_spanned, winnow 0.5), blinc_text unicode-linebreak and unicode-bidi, blinc_platform_desktop objc2 and objc2-foundation (and the windows entry), blinc_layout nom, blinc_core thiserror, blinc_paint bytemuck/smallvec/tracing, blinc_animation smallvec, blinc_gpu slotmap. Keep blinc_gpu's wgpu-hal and gpu-alloc: they are documented version pins.\n2. wgpu features per target: blinc_gpu's desktop preset asks for vulkan-portability on every desktop OS, so macOS compiles ash and the Vulkan backend it never uses (ash 8.2 s, 5 MB source). Select backends per target with target-specific dependency features (Metal on macOS, DX12 on Windows, Vulkan on Linux), and set default-features = false on blinc_app's wgpu. blinc_gpu's direct naga dependency is only used in tests: make it a dev-dependency (wgpu still brings naga).\n3. SVG is not optional in blinc_app: blinc_svg pulls usvg, resvg, tiny-skia and a second rustybuzz (0.18, the unmaintained one) into every build, about 10 exclusive crates. Make it a feature that the facade's existing svg feature turns on, and consider a small icon-path parser over lyon (already a dependency) for the icon case.\n4. image is used for PNG decode only: it brings moxcms, pxfm and others, 7 exclusive crates, 1.6 MB download, 8 MB source, about 12 s CPU. blinc_image already has one decoder per format; use the png crate directly, with other formats behind features.\n5. regex is used by the code widget's highlighter and one more call site (4 crates, 6 MB source, 2.3 s): replace with small lexers, or put the code widget's highlighter behind a feature.\n6. Duplicate objc2 stacks: winit 0.30 uses objc2 0.5, arboard and Blinc use 0.6. Options: wait for winit 0.31, or write the macOS and Windows clipboard directly and keep arboard for Linux only.\n7. Font stack: rustybuzz (unmaintained, see 7f644d9), ttf-parser, swash (read-fonts 0.22 and skrifa 0.22), fontdb. harfrust 0.15 needs read-fonts 0.46 while swash 0.2.10 allows skrifa up to 0.44, so expect a second read-fonts until they align; decide whether swash stays as the rasterizer or glyph outlines come from skrifa directly.\n8. winit and wgpu stay: replacing them means per-OS windowing and three GPU backends, which is not leaner.\n\nNot measured: Linux and Windows compile times, release-profile times, and end-user binary size.","files":null}]}